      "explanation": "To find a lower bound, rewrite the expression as a square plus a constant. The first two terms match \\((x-3)^2=x^2-6x+9\\), so\n\\[\nx^2-6x+13=(x-3)^2+4.\n\\]\nFor every real \\(x\\), the square \\((x-3)^2\\) is nonnegative. Hence the expression is at least \\(4\\), and it equals \\(4\\) when \\(x=3\\). Its minimum is therefore \\(4\\), choice A.",
